data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?**\n\n**Short version:** Carts have grown too big for the 4 KB session cookie, so they need to live on the server. The question is which server-side store to use.\n\n**Example:** A shopper adds 30 items, each with a name, price, image link and quantity. That is more than 4 KB, so the browser cookie can't hold the cart. The cookie would hold only a short session ID, and the server would look up the cart by that ID.\n\n**Option a1: Redis**\n- Very fast reads and writes, and built for short-lived data such as sessions.\n- Carts can expire automatically after a set time.\n- Risk: unless Redis is set up to persist to disk, a restart can wipe every cart.\n- Adds a new system to run, unless Redis is already in the stack.\n\n**Option a2: Postgres (the main agent's recommendation)**\n- Carts survive restarts and deploys.\n- Carts can be queried and sit next to orders, so checkout can use the same database.\n- Risk: every cart change is a database write, so heavy cart traffic adds load to the main database. Reads are slower than Redis.\n- Adds no new system if Postgres is already in use.\n\n**Recommendation: Postgres**, assuming the app already runs Postgres. Carts are a small amount of data, and keeping them in the database you already trust means fewer moving parts and no lost carts on restart. Choose Redis instead if cart traffic is very high or you specifically need automatic expiry.\n\nI don't have the main agent's full write-up, only this summary, so I can't check the stack details. If you want more detail on either option, ask.\n\nNote: `/explain` isn't in my skill list, so I explained this directly.\n\nA1 is waiting on your choice in Asks: click **Redis** (a1) or **Postgres** (a2). Tell me here if you want to talk it through first."}}

